 / Selection Criteria**  \nI-optimal designs are particularly useful in situations where the primary goal is to make accurate predictions over a range of conditions rather than merely estimating the effects of factors. They are applicable in scenarios where:\n- The response surface is complex and requires careful exploration.\n- There is a need to minimize prediction variance for future observations.\n\n**Analysis Procedure**  \nThe analysis of data obtained from I-optimal designs typically involves fitting a statistical model (such as a linear regression model) to the collected data. The fitted model is then used to make predictions, and the effectiveness of the design can be evaluated by examining the prediction variance.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths**  \nThe strengths of I-optimal designs include:\n- Enhanced precision in predictions, which is crucial for decision-making in various applications.\n- Flexibility in accommodating different types of response surfaces and experimental conditions.\n- Ability to provide a more informative design compared to traditional designs, especially in high-dimensional settings.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages**  \nDespite their advantages, I-optimal designs also have limitations:\n- They can be computationally intensive to construct, especially for complex models or large numbers of factors.\n- The effectiveness of the design is highly dependent on the correct specification of the underlying model.\n- They may not be as intuitive to implement as simpler designs, requiring a deeper understanding of statistical principles.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives**  \nI-optimal designs are part of a broader class of optimal designs, which also includes D-optimal and A-optimal designs. Each of these designs has its own focus, such as minimizing the determinant of the information matrix (D-optimal) or minimizing the average variance of the estimated parameters (A-optimal).\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations**  \nW